
  [1mA. Rule-function arrays (recommended)[0m
  [34mconst[39m loginSchema = {
    [36musername[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mminLength[39m([32m3[39m)],
    [36mpassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mminLength[39m([32m8[39m)],
  };
  Each rule is a small factory returning a [38;5;153m(value) => errorMessage | null[39m function. [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m walks them
  and returns [38;5;153m{ valid, errors: { username: ["..."] } }[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m every rule is independently unit-testable in isolation; a one-off custom rule is just a function, no library
  change. [3mCosts:[0m schemas are code, not data — can't be sent over the wire. [3mComplexity:[0m low.

  [1mB. Data-only descriptors[0m
  [34mconst[39m loginSchema = {
    [36musername[39m: { [36mrequired[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36mminLength[39m: [32m3[39m },
    [36mpassword[39m: { [36mrequired[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36mminLength[39m: [32m8[39m },
  };
  The validator interprets a fixed vocabulary of keys.
  [3mBuys:[0m the most readable schema, JSON-serializable, could later come from a server. [3mCosts:[0m a closed rule set — the
  first rule that doesn't fit the vocabulary forces an escape hatch that reintroduces functions anyway. [3mComplexity:[0m low.

  [1mC. HTML-attribute driven[0m
  Rules live in the markup ([38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mtype="email"[39m, [38;5;153mminlength="8"[39m); the module reads them off the form and normalizes
  the browser's built-in Constraint Validation API into consistent messages. No JS schema per form at all.
  [3mBuys:[0m zero duplication between markup and JS; new forms get validation by writing plain HTML. [3mCosts:[0m rules are limited
  to what HTML expresses, messages are harder to control, and the "pure core" you asked for ends up needing a DOM to do
  anything — which fights your second answer. [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It reads almost identically to B at the call site, but absorbs the rules you explicitly deferred
  (password confirmation, async uniqueness) later without a redesign, and it makes the pure core genuinely testable
  rule-by-rule. I'd rule out C: it's elegant for simple cases but directly undercuts the pure-core/display-helper split
  you chose.

  Does A look right, or do you want B's serializable schema?

